    <briefdescription>
<para>Isoparametric Lagrangian cell geometry (Quad4, Hexa8), a geometry policy for the MPM cells. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Uses the shape functions <formula id="50">$ N_A(\boldsymbol{\xi}) $</formula>,  <formula id="51">$ \boldsymbol{\xi}\in[-1,1]^{n_\mathrm{dim}}
$</formula>, of Marmot::Discretizations::Elements::GeometryElement and the isoparametric map <formula id="52">$ \boldsymbol{X}(\boldsymbol{\xi}) = \sum_A N_A(\boldsymbol{\xi})\,\boldsymbol{X}_A $</formula> with the nodal coordinates <formula id="53">$ \boldsymbol{X}_A $</formula>, and provides the inverse map, the physical gradients and the point location test required by <ref refid="concept_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_cells_1_1_geometry_cell_policy" kindref="compound">GeometryCellPolicy</ref>.</para>
<para>For axis-aligned rectangular (box-shaped) cells, the inverse map <ref refid="class_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_cells_1_1_lagrangian_cell_geometry_1ab8d88cbe9f44623e41d085b21affb8fa" kindref="member">findReferenceCoordinate()</ref> and the point location test <ref refid="class_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_cells_1_1_lagrangian_cell_geometry_1ab9538339aae84bbca7e51b7be05ea33c" kindref="member">isCoordinateInCell()</ref> are exact and cheap (affine map of the bounding box); for distorted cells, the inverse map is computed by Newton&apos;s method, and the point location test uses it.</para>
<para><parameterlist kind="templateparam"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>nDim</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Spatial dimension (2 or 3). </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>nNodes</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Number of nodes (4 in 2D, 8 in 3D). </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
